from pathlib import Path
import json,random
root=Path(__file__).resolve().parents[1]
concepts=[]
def add(lecture,text):
 for line in text.strip().splitlines():
  parts=[s.strip() for s in line.split('|')];assert len(parts)==5,(lecture,line)
  term,d,e,f,n=parts
  concepts.append(dict(lecture=lecture,term=term.lstrip('+'),definition=d,example=e,false=f,note=n,origin='Supplementary foundation' if term.startswith('+') else 'Handout topic, clarified'))
add(1, '''
Systematic research | Planned inquiry using explicit procedures to answer a question through evidence. | A clinician specifies recruitment, measurement and analysis before collecting treatment data. | Research is any opinion expressed confidently by an experienced person. | A defensible conclusion follows a transparent method rather than confidence or anecdote alone.
Evidence-based practice | Integration of research evidence, clinical expertise and client or caregiver perspectives. | An SLP combines trial findings with clinical judgment and family priorities. | Evidence-based practice requires ignoring the client's values whenever a trial exists. | Evidence informs decisions together with expertise, circumstances and the client's priorities.
Clinical expertise | Professional judgment developed through training, practice and critical reflection. | A clinician judges whether a study's procedure fits a particular client's situation. | Clinical expertise makes appraisal of research permanently unnecessary. | Expertise helps interpret and apply evidence rather than replace all external checking.
Internal clinical evidence | Information collected about the individual client's performance or response. | Repeated communication probes show how one client responds during therapy. | Internal evidence means only unpublished rumours about a hospital. | Systematic client observations complement external research and require sound measurement.
External research evidence | Findings obtained from investigations beyond the individual clinical encounter. | A clinician consults published comparative studies before selecting an approach. | Every published finding is automatically applicable to every client. | Appraisal and population fit determine how useful external findings are locally.
Research problem | An issue or uncertainty that motivates systematic investigation. | Limited knowledge about rural access to SLP services motivates a study. | A research problem is the same thing as the final statistical result. | The problem motivates the question; results arise only after investigation.
Research objective | A statement of what a study intends to accomplish. | The study aims to compare vocabulary change between two delivery formats. | An objective guarantees that its expected finding will occur. | An aim guides inquiry without predetermining what the evidence must show.
Research protocol | A written plan for design, participants, procedures, outcomes and analysis. | The team documents how children will be recruited and assessed. | A protocol is merely the reference list added after publication. | Planning supports accountability and makes deviations visible when they occur.
Feasibility | Practical achievability given time, participants, resources, skills and constraints. | A student checks whether enough eligible participants can be recruited this semester. | A scientifically interesting question is automatically feasible in every clinic. | Importance must be balanced against recruitment, resources and ethical constraints.
SMART planning | A planning mnemonic emphasizing specific, measurable, achievable, relevant and time-bound goals. | A project defines one outcome and a realistic collection deadline. | Every qualitative question must be reduced to a numerical endpoint to be legitimate. | SMART can support planning without replacing the logic of qualitative inquiry.
Research timeline | A schedule allocating time to linked stages of a project. | Ethics review is scheduled before recruitment and data collection. | Recruitment should begin before ethical requirements are considered. | Dependencies and realistic approval time belong in the research schedule.
Informed consent | A voluntary decision based on understandable information about participation. | An adult receives accessible information and can decline without losing usual care. | A signature alone proves comprehension and freedom from pressure. | Consent is an ongoing communication process, not just a signed form.
Confidentiality | Protecting identifiable participant information against unauthorized disclosure. | Recordings are stored with restricted access and coded identifiers. | Coding a recording makes recognition of the speaker impossible in all circumstances. | Voice and contextual details can remain identifying despite removal of a name.
Privacy | Control over access to a person and their personal circumstances. | Interviews are conducted where others cannot overhear sensitive accounts. | Privacy concerns only whether the final bibliography is alphabetized. | Protecting the encounter and protecting stored information are related but distinct.
Anonymity | A condition where identity is not known or cannot reasonably be linked to responses. | An unlinked survey collects no identifiers or recognizable contextual details. | All confidential interview studies are necessarily anonymous to the interviewer. | An interviewer can know identities while still maintaining confidentiality.
Beneficence | An ethical commitment to promote benefits and minimize avoidable harm. | Researchers reduce unnecessary burden while evaluating potential scientific value. | Beneficence requires promising direct improvement to every participant. | Potential benefit does not justify misleading promises or disproportionate risk.
Justice | Fair distribution of research burdens and benefits and equitable selection. | Recruitment criteria are justified rather than excluding a group merely for convenience. | Justice means enrolling only the easiest-to-reach clients in every study. | Fair inclusion requires scientific and ethical justification, not identical treatment regardless of needs.
Ethics review | Independent assessment of a study's ethical acceptability before relevant activities begin. | A research committee reviews the protocol before recruitment. | Approval removes the team's responsibility to monitor participant welfare. | Ethical duties continue after review, and important changes may require further review.
+Accessible consent and assent | Adapting consent communication and seeking developmentally appropriate agreement where applicable. | A team uses supported communication and appropriate child assent procedures. | A communication impairment automatically proves inability to decide about research. | Capacity must be assessed appropriately; language difficulty alone does not establish incapacity.
Prevalence | The proportion of a defined population with a condition at a specified time or period. | A school survey estimates how many pupils currently meet stated criteria. | Prevalence counts only newly occurring cases during follow-up. | Existing cases define prevalence; new occurrence defines incidence.
Incidence | New occurrence of a condition among people at risk over a specified period. | Researchers follow initially unaffected pupils to identify newly arising cases. | Incidence and prevalence always have the same numerator. | Define the at-risk population and time interval or person-time explicitly.
Critical appraisal | Structured evaluation of a study's validity, relevance and interpretation. | A reader checks allocation, measures, missing data and applicability. | Peer review guarantees that critical appraisal is no longer needed. | Publication does not remove the need to evaluate methods and limitations.
Mean | The sum of numerical values divided by their number. | Scores 2, 4 and 9 have a mean of 5. | The mean is necessarily equal to the most frequent observation. | Outliers can pull the mean away from the middle of the ordered data.
Median | The middle ordered value, or the average of two middle values for an even count. | The median of 1, 2 and 12 is 2. | The median must change greatly whenever one extreme observation increases. | The median is often less sensitive to extreme values than the mean.
Mode | The most frequently occurring value or category. | Diagnosis B occurs more often than any other category. | The mode can only be calculated for ratio-scale measurements. | Frequencies can identify a mode for nominal categories too.
Variability | The spread of observations rather than their central location. | Two groups have the same mean but very different score ranges. | Equal means guarantee that every participant has a similar score. | Range, variance and standard deviation describe different aspects of spread.
Statistical significance | A decision relative to a prespecified threshold for a test under its model. | A prespecified two-sided test returns p below the selected alpha. | A small p value is the probability that the null hypothesis is true. | A p value concerns data at least as extreme under the null model and assumptions.
Confidence interval | An interval produced by a procedure with stated long-run coverage under assumptions. | A 95% procedure covers a fixed population parameter in 95% of repeated samples. | A computed frequentist 95% interval assigns a 95% probability to its fixed parameter. | Interpret coverage through the procedure; also consider the interval's width and assumptions.
Clinical importance | The practical value of a change for functioning, participation or client priorities. | A family reports that gains improve everyday classroom communication. | Any statistically significant difference must be clinically meaningful. | Consider magnitude, uncertainty, everyday impact and client judgment together.
Dissemination | Sharing findings through appropriate reports, presentations or other channels. | Researchers report a null finding with its limitations. | Only positive results should be shared with the research community. | Transparent dissemination reduces selective visibility of favourable findings.
IMRaD structure | Organization of a report into introduction, methods, results and discussion. | A paper separates procedures from findings and interpretation. | The discussion is the appropriate place to hide unreported data-collection methods. | Clear sections help readers distinguish what was planned, observed and inferred.
Peer review | Evaluation of a manuscript by relevant experts to inform editorial decisions. | Reviewers identify weaknesses and request justified revisions. | Acceptance certifies that every published conclusion is permanently correct. | Peer review can improve a report but does not eliminate errors or uncertainty.
Grant proposal | A request for support justified by a research plan, resources and intended contribution. | A budget explains the need for assessment and participant-travel costs. | A persuasive proposal can replace a feasible research method. | Fundability depends on scientific value, feasibility and a justified use of resources.
Research collaboration | Coordination of complementary expertise and responsibilities in a project. | An SLP and statistician plan outcome analysis together. | Collaboration permits leaving authorship and data responsibilities undefined. | Agree roles, communication, data access and appropriate credit in advance.
Outcome monitoring | Systematic measurement of progress against defined goals. | A clinician repeats a specified functional communication measure. | Improvement observed once proves that the intervention caused it. | Repeated monitoring documents change, while causal attribution needs suitable design.
''')
add(2, '''
Research approach | A broad orientation such as quantitative, qualitative or mixed methods. | A project combines numerical comparison with interpretation of caregiver accounts. | A questionnaire is automatically the entire philosophical approach of a study. | Approach, design and specific procedures operate at distinguishable levels.
Research design | The organized strategy connecting a question to evidence and inference. | A randomized comparison specifies how treatment groups will be formed and measured. | Design means only choosing a font for the results table. | Design structures the inquiry rather than merely its presentation.
Research method | A specific procedure for collecting, analyzing or interpreting evidence. | A researcher uses semi-structured interviews and an explicit coding process. | Using interviews always determines one unique methodology. | The same procedure can serve different designs when used for different purposes.
Quantitative approach | Inquiry using numerical measurement to describe patterns or test relationships. | Researchers compare mean intelligibility scores between defined groups. | Every quantitative study necessarily manipulates a treatment. | Numerical studies can be observational, descriptive or experimental.
Qualitative approach | Inquiry interpreting meanings, experiences and processes within context. | Interviews explore how adults experience workplace communication barriers. | Qualitative research has no systematic procedures or need for evidence. | Rigorous qualitative work makes sampling, analysis and interpretation transparent.
Mixed methods | Intentional integration of quantitative and qualitative components. | Interviews help explain unexpected differences found in a survey. | Merely adding an unrelated quotation to a statistical report ensures integration. | State how components connect and what combined inference they support.
Induction | Reasoning from observations toward patterns or more general accounts. | Repeated interview patterns inform the development of explanatory themes. | Induction begins by proving a fixed hypothesis without examining observations. | Inductive interpretations remain open to contrary cases and refinement.
Deduction | Reasoning from a theory or proposition toward testable expectations. | A model predicts a relationship that a study then investigates. | A predicted relationship is true simply because a theory predicts it. | Evidence can challenge predictions as well as support them.
Convergent mixed-methods design | Collection and comparison of complementary components within a coordinated phase. | Survey findings and interviews are integrated around common research questions. | Convergent research requires ignoring disagreement between components. | Divergence can reveal context, measurement or sampling differences worth investigating.
Explanatory sequential design | A quantitative phase followed by qualitative inquiry to explain its findings. | Interviews explore why one clinic's numerical satisfaction scores were unusually low. | Explanatory sequential work always begins with qualitative tool development. | The explanatory purpose links the second phase to the first results.
Exploratory sequential design | Qualitative exploration followed by quantitative development or testing. | Interviews inform a questionnaire that is subsequently evaluated. | Exploratory sequential work begins by treating a final questionnaire as already complete. | Initial interpretation can shape later constructs, measures and hypotheses.
Meta-inference | A conclusion developed through integration across study components. | Numerical uptake patterns and interview barriers jointly explain service access. | Meta-inference means reporting two disconnected result sections without interpretation. | Explain what the combined evidence adds beyond either component alone.
Randomized controlled trial | An intervention comparison assigning participants or clusters by a random process. | Eligible participants are randomly allocated to two therapy formats. | Random assignment guarantees identical baseline characteristics in every small trial. | Randomization balances groups in expectation; chance imbalance can still occur.
Comparison group | A group providing an alternative condition against which an intervention is evaluated. | One group receives an established active therapy rather than the new format. | Every comparison group must receive no support of any kind. | Usual care, active alternatives and other justified comparators are possible.
Quasi-experimental design | An intervention evaluation lacking random assignment in the relevant comparison. | Existing therapy groups receive different programmes. | Calling a study quasi-experimental removes all concern about confounding. | Alternative explanations require explicit design and analytical attention.
Single-case experimental design | Repeated measurement with planned phase changes supporting within-case inference. | Treatment begins at different planned times across participants. | Any descriptive report about one client is automatically a controlled experiment. | Demonstration of experimental control distinguishes it from an ordinary case report.
Baseline phase | Repeated observation before the relevant intervention phase. | Several probes document stable performance before treatment begins. | A single baseline value always establishes a stable trajectory. | Multiple observations help assess trend and variability before interpreting change.
Multiple-baseline design | Staggered intervention introduction across participants, behaviours or settings. | Three participants begin therapy after different baseline lengths. | Multiple baseline requires withdrawing an effective treatment in every case. | Staggered replication can test change without relying on withdrawal.
Case study | An intensive investigation of a bounded case or context. | Detailed records examine one individual's communication and service history. | A case study necessarily establishes a treatment's causal effect. | A case study can generate insight without experimental control.
Survey research | Systematic collection of information from respondents about characteristics or experiences. | SLPs complete a structured questionnaire on service barriers. | A survey must always sample thousands of people to qualify as research. | Sampling, questions and response patterns determine what can be inferred.
Cross-sectional design | Observation of variables within a defined time window without individual follow-up for change. | Researchers compare age groups during one school term. | Age-group differences prove how each individual child develops over time. | Cohort differences and confounding can complicate developmental interpretations.
Longitudinal design | Repeated observation of participants or units across time. | The same children complete language assessments on three occasions. | Following people over time automatically eliminates every causal ambiguity. | Temporal order helps, but confounding, measurement and attrition still matter.
Correlational design | Investigation of association without assigning the relevant exposure or predictor. | Family education and vocabulary are measured rather than experimentally assigned. | Correlation alone establishes that one variable causes the other. | Confounding and reverse direction remain possible explanations.
Retrospective investigation | Inquiry using information about events already occurring before study initiation. | Records of earlier assessment and outcomes are reviewed. | Retrospective studies cannot contain quantitative measurements. | Historical data can be numerical but may have incomplete or inconsistent recording.
Prospective investigation | Planning measurement of events or outcomes as follow-up unfolds. | A newly enrolled group is assessed during the next year. | Prospective follow-up necessarily means random treatment assignment. | Time direction and allocation mechanism are different design dimensions.
Cohort study | Follow-up of a defined group to investigate outcomes and exposures. | A birth cohort is followed for later language outcomes. | A cohort must consist only of people receiving one experimental treatment. | Cohorts can be defined by birth period, setting or other characteristics.
+Case-control study | A design selecting outcome-defined cases and controls to compare prior exposures. | Children with and without an outcome are compared on earlier documented factors. | Case-control studies select groups solely by randomized therapy assignment. | Selection by outcome distinguishes case-control logic from exposure-defined follow-up.
Meta-analysis | Statistical combination of sufficiently compatible effect estimates from multiple studies. | Comparable trials contribute estimates to a pooled analysis. | Any review containing several papers is automatically a meta-analysis. | Pooling requires justified compatibility, appropriate models and attention to bias.
Internal validity | Credibility of conclusions about the relationship studied within a design. | A trial reduces alternative explanations through allocation and standardized assessment. | Internal validity means findings apply to every culture and service setting. | Applicability beyond the study is an external-validity question.
External validity | The extent to which inferences apply to relevant populations and settings beyond the study. | A reader considers fit for Urdu-speaking clients in a local clinic. | A tightly controlled study automatically generalizes everywhere. | Assess the match between study participants, procedures and intended use.
Allocation concealment | Preventing advance knowledge of upcoming treatment assignments during enrolment. | Recruiters cannot predict which group the next eligible participant will enter. | Allocation concealment is identical to hiding results from participants after testing. | Concealment protects the allocation process; blinding concerns knowledge after allocation.
Blinded assessment | Keeping outcome assessors unaware of relevant group assignments where feasible. | A rater evaluates coded speech samples without knowing treatment group. | Blinding an assessor always guarantees that recruitment was random. | Blinding and randomization address different sources of bias.
Attrition | Loss of participants or observations during study follow-up. | More participants leave one intervention arm before the final assessment. | Dropout is harmless whenever the initial sample was large. | Differential or outcome-related loss can bias the comparison.
Treatment fidelity | The extent to which an intervention is delivered as specified. | Session checks document whether planned procedures and dosage were implemented. | Naming a programme proves it was implemented consistently. | Fidelity helps distinguish the intervention plan from what actually occurred.
+Replication | Repeating an investigation to examine the robustness of a finding. | Another team tests the same intervention logic in a different setting. | A study is worthless simply because an earlier study asked a similar question. | Justified replication can test reliability, context and boundary conditions.
''')
add(3, '''
Variable | A characteristic that can differ across units, conditions or time. | Participants differ in age and vocabulary score. | A characteristic is a variable only when experimentally manipulated. | Variables can be measured, classified or manipulated depending on the design.
Construct | A theoretically meaningful concept represented through observations or indicators. | Communication participation is represented through a specified measure. | A construct and one questionnaire item are necessarily identical. | A measure operationalizes a concept without exhausting every possible interpretation.
Conceptual definition | An explanation of what a concept means in the study. | The protocol explains what it means by functional communication. | A conceptual definition is only a list of software commands. | Meaning should be clarified before selecting its measurement procedure.
Operational definition | A precise specification of how a variable is measured or manipulated. | Intelligibility is the percentage of target words transcribed correctly by specified listeners. | Saying that communication is good provides a reproducible operational definition. | Specify procedures, units, timing and relevant scoring rules.
Independent variable | An assigned or explanatory factor examined in relation to an outcome. | In an experiment, two therapy-intensity conditions are assigned. | Every independent variable in every study has been randomized. | Observational predictors are not automatically manipulated causes.
Dependent variable | The response or outcome measured in a specified analysis. | Correct target productions after training form the outcome. | The dependent variable is necessarily the treatment assigned by the researcher. | A variable's role depends on the research question and model.
Mediator | A variable representing a proposed pathway linking a predictor to an outcome. | A programme may improve caregiver practice, which then relates to child outcomes. | A mediator can never be measured directly or through indicators. | Mechanisms can be measured; causal mediation requires stronger assumptions than association alone.
Moderator | A variable associated with differences in a relationship across its levels. | Treatment effects are compared across age groups through an interaction. | A moderator must lie between treatment and outcome on a causal pathway. | Moderation asks when or for whom a relationship differs, unlike mediation's pathway question.
Controlled variable | A factor deliberately held constant or addressed analytically in a study. | Testing instructions are standardized across participants. | Controlling one factor eliminates all threats to validity. | State what was controlled and how; residual problems may remain.
Confounder | A factor creating an alternative explanation through its relationship with exposure and outcome. | Initial severity differs by chosen treatment and predicts later scores. | Any variable measured after treatment is automatically a safe confounder to adjust for. | Adjustment needs a justified model; indiscriminate control can introduce bias.
Covariate | A variable included in an analytical model alongside factors of primary interest. | Baseline score is included in an outcome model. | A covariate is guaranteed to be a cause merely because it appears in a regression. | Covariate is a model-role label, not proof of a causal relationship.
Extraneous variation | Variation from factors outside the primary relationship being investigated. | Different testing environments contribute unrelated score fluctuation. | Every extraneous factor is necessarily a confounder. | Extraneous variation may add noise without systematically distorting the focal relationship.
Nominal scale | Categories without an inherent quantitative order. | Diagnostic categories are coded 1, 2 and 3 for identification. | Coding categories with numbers makes their arithmetic mean clinically meaningful. | Numeric labels identify groups; frequencies and appropriate categorical analyses remain possible.
Ordinal scale | Ordered categories whose numerical distances are not established as equal. | Mild, moderate, severe and profound ratings are ranked. | The difference between adjacent severity categories must always be identical. | Order is justified while equal intervals require additional evidence.
Interval scale | Equal-interval measurement without a meaningful absolute zero. | Celsius temperature permits meaningful differences but not simple temperature ratios. | Forty degrees Celsius is twice as hot as twenty by a valid scale ratio. | Absence of a true zero prevents that direct ratio interpretation.
Ratio scale | Equal-interval measurement with a meaningful zero supporting ratios. | A 200-millisecond duration is twice a 100-millisecond duration. | All numerical test scores are necessarily ratio-scale measures of ability. | The property concerns the measurement scale, not the presence of printed numbers.
Decibel scale | A logarithmic representation relative to a specified reference. | Sound level is reported in dB relative to a reference intensity. | A value of 0 dB necessarily means complete physical absence of sound. | Underlying physical intensity and its logarithmic dB expression have different arithmetic properties.
Discrete variable | A numerical variable taking separate countable values in its specified form. | An assessment counts the number of errors in a fixed task. | An error count must include every fractional real number between zero and one. | Counts differ from continuous measurements even when both use numbers.
Continuous variable | A variable conceptually varying along a continuum within a range. | Response duration can vary by fractions of a second. | Rounding a recorded duration proves the underlying quantity is categorical. | Recording precision and the conceptual nature of the quantity are distinct.
Reliability | Consistency or precision of measurement under specified conditions. | Independent raters produce similar scores from the same speech samples. | A highly consistent measure must measure the intended construct validly. | Consistency alone does not establish the appropriateness of an interpretation.
Validity of measurement | Support for the intended interpretation and use of scores. | Evidence is examined for using a measure with the target language group. | A test valid for one purpose is automatically valid for every population and use. | Validation concerns particular interpretations, populations and decisions.
Inter-rater reliability | Consistency or agreement across raters under a specified scoring procedure. | Two trained raters score the same recorded samples. | Rater agreement is assessed by comparing completely unrelated tasks. | Match the reliability or agreement statistic to scale and purpose.
Test-retest reliability | Stability of measurements across occasions when the target is expected to remain stable. | A stable skill is assessed twice under comparable conditions. | True therapeutic change must be counted as measurement unreliability. | Expected change and measurement error must be distinguished when evaluating stability.
Measurement responsiveness | A measure's capacity to detect relevant change in the construct. | A probe captures small gains over a treatment interval. | A strong diagnostic classification tool is automatically sensitive to short-term change. | Suitability for diagnosis does not ensure responsiveness as an outcome measure.
Demographic variables | Characteristics describing participants' backgrounds. | Age, education and language history are recorded. | A demographic label determines every individual's ability without further evidence. | Group descriptors do not justify stereotypes or deterministic conclusions.
Speech production measures | Indicators of articulation, fluency, voice or related speech behaviours. | A specified speech task supplies accuracy and disfluency counts. | A speech measure needs no description of task or scoring rules. | Task, unit, sampling window and scoring affect interpretation.
Language performance measures | Indicators of comprehension or production across linguistic domains. | A narrative task records defined grammatical and discourse outcomes. | One vocabulary score exhaustively measures every aspect of language. | Language is multidimensional; choose outcomes aligned with the question.
Phonological processing | Processing and manipulation of speech-sound information. | A task asks a participant to delete an initial phoneme. | Phonological processing is identical to naming written font types. | Sound-based tasks require attention to language and task demands.
Executive function | Higher-order regulation such as inhibition, shifting and goal-directed control. | A study examines switching demands during a communication task. | Executive function refers only to how loudly a person speaks. | Define the particular component rather than treating the label as one simple score.
Working memory | Temporary maintenance and manipulation of information. | A task requires retaining verbal material while processing it. | Working memory is identical to a lifetime store of vocabulary definitions. | Distinguish short-term processing demands from long-term lexical knowledge.
Processing speed | The rate at which specified information is processed or responses are produced. | Word-recognition latency is measured in milliseconds. | Faster responses always prove greater accuracy on every task. | Speed and accuracy can trade off; task conditions matter.
Sensory variables | Characteristics of sensory access or processing relevant to the task. | Hearing access and visual support are considered in assessment design. | Normal peripheral hearing proves every auditory-processing task is unaffected. | Sensory access, processing and task performance are related but not identical.
Cross-cultural measurement | Assessment attentive to language history, norms and the intended interpretation. | An English measure is evaluated before use with Urdu-speaking participants. | A linguistic difference from English is sufficient evidence of disorder. | Cultural and linguistic fit must be considered to avoid misinterpretation.
Acquisition outcome | Initial learning of a targeted skill during intervention. | A child improves on trained words within sessions. | Improvement on trained material proves transfer to every untrained setting. | Acquisition is distinct from generalization beyond the training context.
Generalization outcome | Transfer to untrained items, contexts or communication partners. | Newly learned productions occur in untreated conversation samples. | Generalization is established only by repeating the same trained list. | Include untrained material or relevant new contexts in the measurement plan.
Maintenance outcome | Retention of gains over time beyond the relevant intervention phase. | Follow-up probes examine whether learned skills persist. | A posttest immediately after treatment establishes long-term maintenance. | Specify the follow-up interval and subsequent intervention exposure.
Comorbidity | Co-occurrence of additional conditions that may affect interpretation or participation. | A study records relevant co-occurring attention or hearing conditions. | All individuals with one diagnosis have identical co-occurring conditions. | Report relevant heterogeneity without assuming a universal profile.
Developmental variables | Age- or experience-related characteristics relevant to change and measurement. | Researchers consider age and prior language-learning opportunities. | Every child must reach every language milestone on one exact day. | Development varies; age ranges are guides rather than deterministic diagnostic rules.
+Missing data | Values not observed for measurements planned in a study. | Some participants miss the final language assessment. | Replacing every missing outcome with zero is always an unbiased solution. | The reasons and analytical handling of missingness affect interpretation.
+Measurement error | Difference or uncertainty arising from imperfect observation of a quantity. | Small score fluctuations reflect rater or task variability around stable ability. | Every observed score difference necessarily reflects true developmental change. | Assess error relative to the size and purpose of the change being interpreted.
''')
add(4, '''
Target population | The group to which the research aims to relate its conclusions. | A study targets primary schoolchildren in a defined district. | The target population is necessarily identical to everyone attending one clinic. | Define intended scope before deciding how recruitment will occur.
Accessible population | Members practically reachable through the study's setting and procedures. | Participating schools provide the accessible pool. | Accessibility by itself establishes complete coverage of the target population. | Distinguish the intended population from those available through recruitment.
Sample | Units actually selected or participating in a study. | One hundred eligible children contribute observations. | A sample is always every member of the intended population. | A subset may or may not adequately represent the intended target.
Sampling frame | The operational list or structure from which selection is made. | A school enrolment list supplies the frame. | A frame is guaranteed to contain every eligible person without omissions. | Undercoverage, duplicates and outdated entries can affect selection.
Census | An attempt to study every member of a defined population. | All eligible employees in a small service are invited and assessed. | Studying most eligible members is automatically a complete census. | Coverage and nonresponse still need documentation.
Probability sampling | Selection using a random mechanism with known nonzero inclusion probabilities. | Listed eligible members enter a specified random selection procedure. | Every probability method requires equal probabilities for all members. | Known probabilities can differ across strata or stages.
Simple random sampling | Random selection giving each possible fixed-size subset equal probability in the standard definition. | A random generator selects unique IDs from a complete list. | Interviewing whoever volunteers first is simple random sampling. | A specified chance mechanism distinguishes random selection from casual choice.
Systematic sampling | Selection at a fixed interval after an appropriate random start. | From 300 names, a random start precedes selection of every twentieth. | Choosing every twentieth without considering the start and ordering removes all bias risks. | Periodicity in the frame can compromise the intended sampling properties.
Sampling interval | The spacing used in a systematic design, commonly based on population divided by sample size. | Three hundred divided by fifteen gives an interval of twenty. | The interval for 300 names and 15 selections is necessarily fifteen. | Describe the actual procedure when ratios are not integers or frames are complex.
Stratified random sampling | Partitioning into strata and randomly selecting within each stratum. | Students are randomly sampled separately within each academic year. | Setting group quotas without random within-group selection guarantees stratified random sampling. | Random selection within strata distinguishes this design from ordinary quota recruitment.
Proportional allocation | Assigning stratum sample sizes in proportion to population shares. | A group containing 40% of the population supplies 40% of selections. | Proportional allocation requires equal numbers from unequal-size strata. | Multiply total intended sample by the stratum's population proportion.
Disproportionate allocation | Selecting strata at unequal sampling fractions for a stated purpose. | A small subgroup is oversampled to support subgroup analysis. | Oversampling a subgroup necessarily invalidates every possible population estimate. | Appropriate weights and design-aware analysis can address unequal selection.
Stratum | A defined subgroup from which participants are separately selected in a stratified design. | Academic year defines the strata in a student survey. | A stratum is defined as one randomly chosen entire school in a cluster sample. | Strata and clusters serve different roles in the sampling plan.
Cluster | A naturally or operationally grouped set of units used in cluster sampling. | Schools act as clusters of pupils. | Every real cluster must perfectly reproduce the whole population's composition. | Mini-population is a teaching analogy, not a guaranteed feature of actual clusters.
Cluster sampling | Random selection of groups followed by study of units in those selected groups. | Twenty schools are selected from a district's school list. | Ordinary cluster sampling always selects individuals from every single school. | Sampling some clusters distinguishes it from sampling within every stratum.
One-stage cluster design | Selection of clusters followed by inclusion of all eligible units within them. | All eligible pupils in selected schools are assessed. | One-stage cluster sampling requires a further random sample of pupils within every selected school. | Further within-cluster selection creates an additional stage.
Multistage sampling | Selection through linked stages, such as areas, schools and pupils. | Schools are selected first and children sampled within them. | Multistage sampling means repeating the same questionnaire after therapy. | Stages concern selection levels rather than measurement occasions.
Convenience sampling | Recruitment primarily based on accessibility and availability. | The available clients at one nearby clinic are invited. | A convenience sample becomes random merely because participants have different ages. | Diversity alone does not establish a probability selection mechanism.
Purposive sampling | Deliberate selection of information-rich cases meeting a study's purpose. | Caregivers with relevant service experience are selected for interviews. | Researcher judgment guarantees minimal error and population representativeness. | Justify why selected experiences illuminate the question and acknowledge limits.
Consecutive sampling | Inclusion of every eligible accessible case encountered over a defined period until a stated endpoint. | All qualifying clinic referrals during six months are invited. | Consecutive sampling means moving between arbitrary groups only after each is analyzed. | Consecutive inclusion requires a defined recruitment stream and consistent eligibility rules.
Quota sampling | Nonprobability recruitment until specified subgroup numbers are filled. | Researchers recruit until each diagnosis quota is reached. | Matching subgroup proportions establishes random selection within those groups. | Quotas can control composition without removing within-group selection bias.
Snowball sampling | Chain-referral recruitment through participants' networks. | Initial participants suggest other eligible people with a rare condition. | Snowball sampling gives every unknown population member a known equal chance. | Network dependence and initial contacts can shape who becomes reachable.
Inclusion criteria | Conditions defining eligibility for participation. | Age range and language background are specified before enrolment. | Inclusion criteria may be changed secretly to obtain a preferred result. | Criteria should match the question and be applied transparently.
Exclusion criteria | Specified reasons an otherwise relevant person is not included. | A safety-related condition excludes an individual from one protocol. | Exclusions require no ethical or scientific justification. | Avoid unjustified restrictions and report their implications for applicability.
Sampling bias | Systematic distortion caused by how a sample differs from its target. | Recruitment excludes families unable to attend during working hours. | Increasing sample size always eliminates systematic selection bias. | More biased observations can produce a more precise biased estimate.
Sampling error | Random variation of sample estimates across repeated probability samples. | Two valid random samples give slightly different prevalence estimates. | Sampling error is identical to deliberately selecting only favourable cases. | Chance variability differs from systematic selection distortion.
Nonresponse bias | Distortion when response is related to characteristics important to the research. | Families facing the greatest barriers are less likely to return a survey. | A high invitation count guarantees absence of nonresponse bias. | Evaluate who responded and why nonresponse could matter.
Random sampling versus assignment | A distinction between selecting a sample and allocating enrolled units to conditions. | Convenience recruits are randomly assigned to two treatment groups. | Random treatment allocation proves that recruitment represented the whole population. | Allocation can improve internal comparisons without fixing population sampling.
Sample-size planning | Justifying numbers from precision, effect, design, variability and feasibility. | An estimate allows for clustering and expected loss to follow-up. | Every study needs exactly thirty participants regardless of its question. | No universal number replaces design-specific justification.
Selection transparency | Clear reporting of who was eligible, approached, enrolled and analyzed. | A recruitment account explains exclusions and refusals. | Reporting only the final number fully explains the recruitment process. | Transparent selection allows readers to judge applicability and potential bias.
''')
add(5, '''
Focused research question | An explicit inquiry narrow enough to investigate with suitable evidence. | A question specifies a population, intervention comparison and vocabulary outcome. | A broad topic label alone fully specifies an answerable study. | Clarify what is unknown, for whom, and what evidence could answer it.
Clinical observation | An experience-based starting point that may generate an investigable question. | A clinician notices variable attendance across service formats. | One observed improvement proves general treatment superiority. | Observation motivates investigation without substituting for an appropriate comparison.
Topic refinement | Narrowing a broad interest into a manageable inquiry. | Therapy and language becomes a comparison of defined programmes in one population. | Refinement requires hiding eligibility criteria until after seeing results. | Set boundaries to align the question, methods and feasible scope.
Research gap | An evidence-supported uncertainty or limitation motivating further work. | A review finds conflicting results for a clearly defined outcome. | Any topic not personally encountered by the student is automatically an established gap. | A gap must be justified through relevant searching and appraisal.
Population specification | Stating who the question concerns through relevant characteristics. | Age range, diagnosis and language history define the group of interest. | Diagnosis alone always supplies every eligibility detail needed. | Include characteristics necessary to interpret the question without arbitrary overrestriction.
Intervention specification | Clear description of the programme, delivery and dosage being investigated. | A protocol states session frequency, duration and parent-coaching procedures. | Giving an intervention a fashionable name makes dosage irrelevant. | The assigned procedure must be sufficiently defined for interpretation and replication.
Comparator choice | Selection of the alternative relevant to the question and ethical context. | New treatment is compared with usual care rather than an unrelated group. | A comparator must always be a group of typically developing people. | Active alternatives and usual care answer different questions from no treatment.
Outcome specification | Defining what result is examined and how it will be measured. | Everyday intelligibility is assessed by a specified listener protocol. | Any available test score answers every communication-related question. | Outcome choice must match the construct and the proposed intervention target.
Time specification | Stating relevant intervention, measurement or follow-up intervals. | Outcomes are measured after eight weeks and at later follow-up. | Post-treatment timing can never affect interpretation of an intervention. | Immediate acquisition and later maintenance require different measurement occasions.
Descriptive question | Inquiry about characteristics, frequency or distribution. | What service barriers are reported in the selected clinics? | Every descriptive question requires random assignment to therapy. | Description and causal attribution require different forms of evidence.
Association question | Inquiry about a relationship between measured factors. | Is home-language exposure associated with a specified vocabulary measure? | Including the word association guarantees proof of direction of causation. | Interpret measured relationships in light of design and alternative explanations.
Causal question | Inquiry about effects attributable to a specified intervention or exposure. | Would assigning one service format change a defined outcome? | A causal-sounding sentence makes an uncontrolled design causally conclusive. | The design and assumptions must support the effect being asked about.
Experience question | Inquiry about meaning and lived or reported experience. | How do adults who stutter describe workplace participation? | Every experience question must be answered by comparing numerical group means. | Qualitative approaches can address meaning without forcing it into a treatment-effect format.
Question-design alignment | Matching the kind of inquiry with an appropriate strategy for evidence. | A question about experience leads to contextualized interviews and interpretation. | Choosing a favourite statistical test should always precede defining the research question. | Question, evidence, design and analysis should form a coherent chain.
Question-measure alignment | Selecting observations that actually address the question's construct. | A functional communication question uses an outcome reflecting everyday communication. | Naming an outcome scale proves it measures every intended construct. | Check the intended interpretation and responsiveness of the chosen score.
Question-sample alignment | Recruitment that supplies evidence from the people or cases relevant to the inquiry. | A caregiver-experience study recruits people with the relevant experience. | A large unrelated sample answers a narrowly defined target-population question. | Relevance of the sampled cases matters alongside numbers.
Feasible scope | Limits consistent with accessible participants, resources and time. | A student restricts a proposal to procedures available locally. | An impossible recruitment plan is rescued merely by an impressive title. | Refine scope transparently rather than promising unachievable coverage.
Ethically answerable question | An inquiry that can be investigated without unjustified harm or coercion. | A comparison preserves appropriate usual services and voluntary participation. | Important scientific uncertainty always permits withholding essential care without justification. | The question's value does not override participant protection.
Primary outcome | The main outcome prespecified for a study's central inference. | One vocabulary outcome anchors the trial's sample-size calculation. | A primary outcome may be selected secretly after whichever result has the smallest p value. | Prespecification helps limit selective outcome reporting.
+Secondary outcome | An additional outcome answering a supplementary question. | Participation is measured alongside the main target-skill outcome. | A secondary outcome must be ignored and omitted from the report. | Report planned outcomes with appropriate interpretation of multiplicity and priority.
Directional hypothesis | A prediction specifying the direction of a relationship or difference. | The new format is predicted to increase a prespecified outcome. | Every quantitative hypothesis must avoid stating a direction. | A prediction should be justified and distinguished from the observed result.
Null hypothesis | A specified reference hypothesis for statistical testing. | The model posits no mean difference between conditions. | Failing to reject the null proves exact equality of every relevant effect. | Lack of evidence against a null is not automatic evidence of equivalence.
Effect-size expectation | A justified planning value for the magnitude of a target difference or relationship. | Sample-size planning uses a difference considered substantively important. | Smaller target effects always require fewer observations under otherwise fixed conditions. | Smaller effects generally require more information for comparable power.
Rare-population constraint | Limited access to eligible participants requiring realistic design choices. | A rare-condition study considers repeated-measure or multicentre options. | A rare population licenses inventing participants to reach a target number. | Adapt the question and design honestly to recruitment realities.
Clinical construct caution | Avoiding substitution of a structural or diagnostic construct for a behavioural treatment target. | A speech-coaching study distinguishes intelligibility from structural velopharyngeal function. | Improvement in speech practice automatically proves correction of an anatomical deficit. | Specify what the intervention can plausibly target and measure that construct appropriately.
''')
add(6, '''
Question framework | A mnemonic organizing the components of a researchable inquiry. | A student separates people, treatment, comparison and outcome. | Selecting a framework automatically validates a study's design or conclusion. | Frameworks clarify components; they do not replace methodological judgment.
PICO | Population, Intervention, Comparison and Outcome for a focused intervention question. | Two therapy formats are compared for a defined group and outcome. | The O in PICO means the person observing the test. | The letters identify components, not a mandatory experimental design by themselves.
PICO population | The people or problem targeted by an intervention question. | School-age children with persistent speech sound difficulties form P. | P in PICO is always the professional delivering treatment. | Relevant participant characteristics define the target group.
PICO intervention | The treatment, programme or assigned procedure being evaluated. | Ultrasound biofeedback is the intervention in a comparison question. | Intervention in PICO means the outcome score obtained afterwards. | Describe the procedure separately from the results it is intended to influence.
PICO comparison | The alternative condition used to interpret the intervention. | Traditional articulation therapy provides the comparison format. | Comparison in PICO necessarily means a different outcome instrument. | Different conditions should be measured on interpretable outcome bases.
PICO outcome | The result or construct examined in the intervention question. | Accuracy on a prespecified speech task is the outcome. | A broad aspiration such as better life needs no operational clarification. | Specify the construct and measurement rather than assuming an intention is a measure.
PICOT | PICO expanded with an explicit time element. | A question adds outcomes after ten weeks of treatment. | Adding T establishes that treatment assignment was randomized. | Time clarifies the intended interval without establishing allocation method.
PEO | Population, Exposure and Outcome for exposure-focused or related questions. | Screen-time exposure is related to a defined language outcome without assignment. | PEO requires researchers to assign every naturally occurring exposure. | Its components support observational questions but do not prove causal effects.
PECO | Population, Exposure, Comparator and Outcome. | Higher and lower measured exposure groups are compared on one outcome. | The exposure must always be an experimentally assigned therapeutic programme. | Distinguish exposure comparisons from intervention assignment.
Exposure | A factor or experience encountered rather than necessarily assigned. | Naturally occurring home-language environments are documented. | Calling a factor exposure proves it was measured before the outcome. | Establish time order and measurement separately from the label.
PCC framework | Population, Concept and Context for framing a scoping inquiry. | Evidence on telepractice for adults with aphasia is mapped across community settings. | Both C letters in PCC mean comparison groups. | In this framework the C components are concept and context.
PCC concept | The central idea, phenomenon, intervention or measure being mapped. | Communication-participation measures form the concept in a scoping review. | Concept in PCC must always be a randomized placebo. | A scoping concept can encompass types of evidence or service practice.
PCC context | The setting, geographical or sociocultural circumstances defining a scoping inquiry. | South Asian community services provide the review context. | Context and sample size are identical components. | Define relevant environmental boundaries rather than confusing them with participant count.
SPIDER | Sample, Phenomenon of Interest, Design, Evaluation and Research type. | Caregiver experience is explored through interviews within a qualitative inquiry. | SPIDER was created solely to replace every quantitative intervention framework. | It supports qualitative or mixed evidence questions; suitability depends on the purpose.
SPIDER sample | The group whose relevant experience or perspective is investigated. | Parents of children receiving a service form the sample element. | S in SPIDER always means the physical clinic setting. | Do not transfer the S definition from SPICE to SPIDER.
Phenomenon of interest | The experience, behaviour or process explored in a SPIDER question. | Navigating telepractice barriers is the phenomenon of interest. | A phenomenon of interest must always be a randomized drug dose. | Experience-focused inquiry does not require an assigned intervention.
SPIDER design | The proposed evidence-generating procedure or study form in this mnemonic. | Semi-structured interviews specify D in a teaching example. | D in SPIDER identifies the final clinical diagnosis alone. | Framework terminology is pragmatic; still distinguish collection procedure from broader methodology.
SPIDER evaluation | Views, experiences, attitudes or other evaluative material being examined. | Parent perceptions of access difficulties specify evaluation. | Evaluation in SPIDER must always be a standardized test score. | Evaluation can capture subjective meaning relevant to the research question.
SPIDER research type | The broad inquiry type represented by R in the framework. | A study is explicitly identified as qualitative. | The R in SPIDER always means the recruitment response rate. | State the inquiry type rather than confusing it with an outcome or statistic.
SPICE | Setting, Perspective, Intervention or Interest, Comparison and Evaluation. | A service question compares group and individual therapy from clients' perspectives. | SPICE stands for Sample, Programme, Instrument, Cost and Experiment. | The corrected mapping distinguishes service setting and stakeholder perspective.
SPICE setting | The service or physical context where a SPICE question is situated. | Outpatient hospital clinics supply S. | S in SPICE necessarily identifies the sample rather than the setting. | Keep framework-specific meanings separate even when initial letters match.
SPICE perspective | The stakeholder viewpoint central to a service question. | Adults with aphasia provide the perspective on communication confidence. | P in SPICE is always the posttest date. | Stakeholder perspective differs from the location of the service.
SPICE comparison and evaluation | The alternative service and criterion for evaluating it in the mnemonic. | Individual therapy is compared with group therapy on confidence. | Comparison and evaluation are interchangeable labels for one element. | Identify what is contrasted separately from the criterion used to judge it.
ECLIPSE | Expectation, Client group, Location, Impact, Professionals and Service. | A service-improvement question identifies goals, users, setting, staff and provision. | ECLIPSE is a statistical test for normally distributed gain scores. | This is a question-structuring tool for service or management issues.
ECLIPSE expectation | The improvement or information sought through the service inquiry. | Improving the screening pathway states the expectation. | Expectation in ECLIPSE proves that improvement already occurred. | A desired improvement is an inquiry goal, not an observed finding.
ECLIPSE client and location | The users of the service and the place it is delivered. | Stroke patients and acute wards specify client group and location. | Client group in ECLIPSE means only the staff delivering the service. | Professionals and client group have distinct roles in this framework.
ECLIPSE impact | The intended difference or consequence of service improvement. | Reduced service delay is the proposed impact. | Impact is identical to the name of the professional team. | State the intended effect separately from staffing and service identity.
ECLIPSE professionals and service | The staff involved and the service being investigated. | Nurses and SLPs deliver a specified screening pathway. | SE in ECLIPSE is the abbreviation for standard error. | SE denotes Service in this mnemonic, not a statistical quantity.
Framework selection | Matching a mnemonic's emphasis to the substantive inquiry. | An evidence-mapping question uses PCC rather than forcing a therapy comparator. | Every research question must use PICO regardless of its purpose. | Choose for fit, while recognizing that frameworks are aids rather than exclusive laws.
Framework versus search strategy | The distinction between structuring a question and constructing a retrieval query. | A PICO question is translated into selected search concept blocks. | Every PICO element must always be searched with AND in every database. | Overly restrictive outcome or comparison blocks can miss relevant evidence.
''')
add(7, '''
Literature review | Critical, organized synthesis of existing scholarship relevant to an inquiry. | A chapter explains agreements, disagreements and methodological limits across studies. | A literature review is complete when titles are listed without interpretation. | Synthesis connects sources to a question rather than merely accumulating citations.
Search concept block | A group of related terms representing one searchable concept. | Alternative names for an intervention are grouped together. | One concept block should always combine unrelated populations and outcomes with OR. | Organize synonyms within concepts before combining different concepts.
Keyword | A free-text term used to retrieve potentially relevant records. | A search includes telepractice as a title or abstract term. | A single keyword guarantees retrieval of every relevant paper. | Include justified variants and complementary indexing terms.
Search synonym | An alternative expression used to improve retrieval for a concept. | Telepractice and teletherapy are considered as alternative search expressions. | All clinically distinct disorders must be treated as exact synonyms in a search. | Retrieval expansion does not establish conceptual interchangeability.
Boolean AND | An intersection operator requiring records to meet combined concepts. | Stuttering AND intervention asks for both concept conditions. | AND normally broadens a fixed database search by accepting either concept. | AND typically narrows relative to either component searched alone.
Boolean OR | A union operator retrieving records meeting either or both alternatives. | Aphasia OR dysphasia retrieves either specified term. | OR means a record containing both alternatives must be excluded. | Boolean OR is ordinarily inclusive rather than exclusive.
Boolean NOT | An exclusion operator removing records that meet a specified condition. | A carefully justified exclusion removes a clearly irrelevant concept. | NOT can never remove a relevant mixed-population study. | Exclusions may discard useful records and should be tested cautiously.
Search parentheses | Grouping that makes the intended Boolean combinations explicit. | Two synonyms joined by OR are grouped before adding an AND block. | Parentheses are only decorative and never affect search logic. | Databases differ in precedence, so explicit grouping improves clarity.
Phrase searching | Searching for words together in a specified phrase pattern. | A database query requests the phrase speech perception. | Quotation marks behave identically in every database and circumstance. | Verify platform-specific phrase rules and test whether relevant records are retrieved.
Truncation | Use of a supported wildcard to retrieve word variants sharing a stem. | A supported child stem search retrieves relevant word endings. | The same truncation symbol and behaviour are guaranteed across all platforms. | Check the database's syntax, limits and effect on automatic mapping.
Controlled vocabulary | A standardized indexing system representing concepts consistently. | A database assigns a recognized subject heading to an indexed article. | Controlled vocabulary consists only of whichever spelling an author used in an abstract. | Index terms complement free-text searching rather than duplicating its exact mechanism.
MeSH | Medical Subject Headings, the NLM controlled vocabulary used for MEDLINE indexing. | A PubMed search combines relevant MeSH headings with title-abstract terms. | Every new PubMed record necessarily has complete MeSH indexing immediately. | Indexing coverage and timing are reasons to combine headings with free text.
MeSH hierarchy | Organization of headings into broader and narrower relationships. | A researcher checks whether narrower headings are included by a search. | MeSH headings form only an unstructured alphabetical list with no conceptual relations. | Understand hierarchical expansion and inspect relevant indexing choices.
Title-abstract field search | Retrieval restricted to specified bibliographic text fields under a platform's syntax. | PubMed uses a title-abstract tag to target text fields. | A title-abstract tag searches every word in every publisher's full article. | Field restrictions define where matching occurs, not full-text access.
PubMed | An NLM search resource for biomedical citations, including MEDLINE and additional records. | A researcher searches biomedical communication-disorder literature. | Every PubMed citation provides free full text and complete MEDLINE indexing. | Citation discovery, indexing status and access to full text are separate matters.
ASHA research resources | Discipline-specific publications and evidence resources for communication sciences. | ASHA journals and evidence summaries inform a speech-language question. | A publisher search alone necessarily covers all worldwide SLP evidence. | Discipline focus is useful but does not replace broader, question-appropriate searching.
Allied-health and education databases | Resources serving domains such as rehabilitation, nursing, psychology and education. | CINAHL, PsycINFO or ERIC supplement a topic-specific search. | All databases cover exactly the same journals and disciplines. | Choose sources for the question and document their contribution.
Citation tracking | Finding related evidence through cited or citing publications. | Reference lists and citing papers identify additional relevant studies. | Citation tracking guarantees identification of all relevant unpublished work. | Record supplementary discovery rather than assuming networks are complete.
Backward citation searching | Examining references cited by an identified publication. | A review's reference list points to earlier relevant studies. | Backward citation searching follows only papers published after the seed article. | It follows cited earlier sources, which must still be checked for eligibility.
Forward citation searching | Identifying later publications citing a known source. | A citation index finds studies that cite an early therapy trial. | Forward citation searching means reading only an article's own bibliography. | Citation coverage depends on the resource and may be incomplete.
Google Scholar searching | Broad scholarly discovery that can complement structured database searches. | A reviewer uses citation links to locate additional material. | Google Scholar alone guarantees a completely reproducible systematic search. | Record queries and limits while recognizing ranking and coverage limitations.
Search log | A record of sources, dates, exact queries, limits and retrieval counts. | The appendix reports each database strategy and search date. | Reporting only the database name makes a search reproducible. | Reproducibility requires enough detail to understand what was actually run.
Database translation | Adapting concepts to each platform's headings, fields and syntax. | A PubMed strategy is revised before being used in CINAHL. | Copying one database's tags unchanged always works correctly everywhere. | Retain conceptual intent while checking each platform's rules.
Search sensitivity | The ability to retrieve relevant material rather than miss it. | Additional justified synonyms recover studies omitted by one narrow term. | A search finding very few irrelevant records must have found every relevant study. | Sensitivity and precision are different retrieval goals.
Search precision | The proportion of retrieved records that are relevant under stated criteria. | More retrieved records meet the review's eligibility criteria after testing refinements. | High precision necessarily proves perfect sensitivity. | A very narrow strategy may appear precise while missing eligible evidence.
Search limits | Restrictions such as language, date or publication type applied to retrieval. | A date restriction is explicitly justified and reported. | English-only restrictions can never affect a review's evidence base. | Limits can introduce exclusions or bias and should match the question.
Reference manager | Software for organizing bibliographic records, citations and related files. | Zotero stores metadata and generates a selected citation format. | Installing a reference manager guarantees every imported detail is accurate. | Check metadata, duplicates and source identity rather than relying on automation alone.
Evidence matrix | A structured comparison table with common information fields across studies. | Rows contain studies and columns summarize design, results and limitations. | An evidence matrix requires copying complete articles into a single cell. | Comparable fields support synthesis across studies rather than file storage alone.
Ethical paraphrasing | Restating an idea accurately in original wording while acknowledging its source. | A student changes structure and expression and cites the original study. | Replacing a few words removes the need to cite another author's idea. | Attribution remains necessary even without a direct quotation.
Plagiarism | Presenting another person's words, ideas or work without appropriate acknowledgment. | A paragraph is copied with no quotation or citation. | A low similarity score proves that all ideas are properly attributed. | Similarity detection and ethical attribution are related but not equivalent checks.
''')
add(8, '''
Narrative review | An interpretive overview whose scope and selection should be explained. | A thesis background connects theories and findings in a stated area. | Narrative reviewing permits deliberately concealing contrary evidence. | Flexibility does not remove the need for fair, critical and transparent reasoning.
Systematic review | Evidence synthesis using explicit, planned methods to answer a defined question. | Eligibility, searches, screening and appraisal are specified before synthesis. | A systematic review necessarily includes a statistical meta-analysis. | A systematic review can synthesize findings without quantitative pooling.
Scoping review | Systematic mapping of evidence types, concepts and gaps around a broader inquiry. | A review charts what AAC services have been studied and where. | Critical appraisal is forbidden in every scoping review. | Appraisal is not mandatory for all scoping purposes but may be justified.
Review protocol | An advance plan describing how a review will be conducted. | Reviewers prespecify eligibility, sources and synthesis approach. | A protocol can be reconstructed silently after results are known with no consequence. | Prespecification and transparent amendments help distinguish planning from selective decisions.
Protocol registration | Public recording of review plans in an appropriate registry or repository. | A team checks the scope of a registry before submitting its protocol. | Every review type is eligible for every registry. | Registry coverage and requirements differ; choose a suitable platform.
PRISMA 2020 | A reporting guideline for systematic reviews with checklists and flow information. | A report explains identification, screening and included studies. | PRISMA is itself a risk-of-bias assessment instrument. | Reporting completeness and methodological quality require different assessments.
PRISMA-ScR | A reporting extension addressing scoping reviews. | A scoping report describes its evidence-charting and selection process. | PRISMA-ScR transforms a mapping review automatically into a treatment-effect meta-analysis. | Use the guideline appropriate to the review's purpose and methods.
SANRA | A structured scale for assessing aspects of narrative review quality. | A reader examines justification, search description and scientific reasoning. | SANRA is a randomized treatment allocation method. | It concerns narrative review assessment rather than intervention assignment.
Deduplication | Identifying and managing repeated records of the same source. | Database exports containing one article twice are merged appropriately. | Every article with a similar topic is a duplicate and should be removed. | Distinct studies on the same topic must not be mistaken for duplicate records.
Title-abstract screening | Initial eligibility assessment using bibliographic summaries. | Reviewers assess whether a record potentially matches the inclusion criteria. | Abstract screening always makes full-text assessment unnecessary. | Uncertain or potentially eligible records commonly proceed to fuller assessment.
Full-text assessment | Examination of the report to determine eligibility and extract sufficient information. | A team records why a retrieved paper fails a prespecified criterion. | Full-text exclusions require no stated reason in a transparent review. | Document exclusions so readers can follow selection decisions.
Independent review | Assessment by reviewers separately before resolving differences. | Two reviewers apply criteria and discuss disagreements. | Two names on a paper prove that screening was conducted independently. | Report the actual screening and conflict-resolution procedure.
Flow-diagram accounting | Transparent reporting of records, reports and studies through selection stages. | Counts distinguish removed duplicates from full-text exclusions. | The number of database records must always equal the number of unique studies. | One study may have multiple reports, and one report may be retrieved repeatedly.
Data extraction | Recording prespecified information from included reports. | Reviewers capture sample, measures, effects and limitations into a form. | Extraction should include only results supporting the reviewer's expectation. | Use consistent fields and verify important information against the source.
Data charting | Organizing information to map the evidence in a scoping review. | A chart summarizes populations, concepts and service contexts. | Data charting always requires estimating one pooled treatment effect. | Charting can map characteristics without inferential pooling.
Risk of bias | Potential systematic deviation of findings from the target truth under a study's methods. | A trial's outcome assessment and missing-data processes are evaluated. | A prestigious journal guarantees low risk of bias in every study. | Judge relevant design features rather than reputation alone.
RoB 2 | A structured tool for assessing risk of bias in randomized trial results. | Reviewers examine domains relevant to an RCT comparison. | RoB 2 is designed only to format Vancouver references. | Match the tool and its version to the study and result being assessed.
ROBINS-I | A tool family for bias assessment in nonrandomized studies of interventions. | An observational treatment comparison is evaluated for confounding and related bias. | A nonrandomized study needs no assessment of confounding. | Use appropriate guidance; the tool is not a guarantee of causal validity.
AMSTAR 2 | Critical appraisal of systematic reviews of randomized or nonrandomized healthcare intervention studies. | A reviewer assesses a review's methods rather than its citation style. | AMSTAR 2 is a replacement name for participant randomization. | Review-level appraisal differs from assessment of individual primary studies.
Design-matched checklist | An appraisal instrument selected for the actual study type and question. | A cohort checklist is chosen for a cohort report. | One generic checklist is perfectly suited to every possible research design. | CASP and JBI offer design-specific resources, not one universal test for everything.
Evidence hierarchy | A broad ordering of designs for particular questions, especially intervention effects. | A robust comparative trial may inform an intervention decision. | A high design label overrides serious bias and poor population relevance. | Quality, question fit and applicability modify what a hierarchy alone suggests.
Thematic synthesis | Organizing evidence around related substantive ideas or patterns. | Studies are compared under access, delivery and participation themes. | Synthesis must consist only of one disconnected paragraph per author. | Link findings and limitations across sources to develop an evidence-based account.
Methodological comparison | Comparing findings in relation to design and measurement differences. | Divergent effects are considered alongside outcome and follow-up differences. | Conflicting findings prove that one author must be dishonest. | Methods, context and uncertainty may explain disagreement without misconduct.
Synthesis paragraph | An argument connecting findings, limitations, contrasts and unresolved questions. | A paragraph identifies agreement, critiques controls and specifies a justified gap. | A synthesis paragraph needs no citations because it discusses several sources. | Attribute the evidence supporting each part of the argument.
Vancouver numbering | A numeric citation convention assigning references in order of first citation. | A source keeps its original number when cited again. | Vancouver requires a new reference number every time the same source reappears. | Reuse the number for the same source and check the required journal variant.
Reference-list order | The sequence of sources required by the selected citation system. | A Vancouver list follows citation numbers rather than alphabetical order. | Every reference system requires the same alphabetical ordering. | Citation style determines ordering and punctuation rules.
Bibliographic verification | Checking authors, title, source, date and identifier against the original publication. | A DOI record is checked before a generated citation is retained. | A plausible-looking DOI proves that the cited claim is supported. | Source existence and support for a claim require separate verification.
Zotero metadata workflow | Importing, checking and organizing bibliographic records before citation. | Records imported by DOI are checked and duplicate entries merged. | Automatic import can never introduce title or author errors. | Clean metadata before relying on generated citations and bibliographies.
Citation-plugin workflow | Linking in-text citations and bibliography through reference-management software. | A document's reference numbering updates after insertion of a source. | A manually pasted number always remains linked to the bibliography automatically. | Preserve the citation fields and verify the final generated output.
Responsible AI assistance | Use of automated support with human verification, privacy protection and accountability. | A researcher checks suggested search terms and verifies each source. | AI-generated citations can be accepted without reading or checking the sources. | Humans remain responsible for accuracy and policy-compliant use.
AI hallucination | Generated content that is false, unsupported or misrepresents a source. | A tool supplies a nonexistent article with a plausible title. | A fluent, detailed summary guarantees faithful reporting of the actual study. | Verify existence, methods and findings at the source before relying on the output.
AI authorship and disclosure | Accountability and transparent reporting of permitted AI assistance under relevant policies. | Authors describe allowed assistance and take responsibility for the work. | An AI system can independently accept research accountability as a human author. | Follow the applicable journal and institutional requirements rather than assume one universal wording.
Confidential data protection | Avoiding unauthorized disclosure of sensitive research information through tools or services. | Identifiable recordings are not uploaded to unapproved systems. | A convenient tool's popularity proves participant data may be uploaded without authorization. | Consent, security, approvals and data-governance requirements still apply.
Citation-network limitations | Restrictions of discovery tools arising from the sources and links they index. | A mapping tool misses a relevant paper absent from its database. | A visually connected citation map proves a search is complete. | Citation maps support discovery but do not replace systematic search documentation.
Scoping review stages | Question definition, evidence identification, selection, charting and synthesis within a stated approach. | A team charts studies before reporting the pattern of evidence. | A scoping review is defined by omitting an explicit selection process. | Consultation and updated methodological refinements should be justified within the chosen approach.
''')
add(9, '''
Question-design-analysis congruence | Coherence between the inquiry, data-generating design and analytical interpretation. | A question about group differences in change uses a justified change-based comparison. | Any available software test answers every pre-post question equivalently. | State the target comparison before selecting a statistical procedure.
Gain score | A change value calculated as posttest minus pretest under the stated convention. | A score increasing from 12 to 18 has gain 6. | Gain is always pretest divided by posttest. | State score direction because a positive increase is not always clinical improvement.
Mean gain | The average of participants' gain scores within a defined group. | Individual changes are summed and divided by the participant count. | Mean gain necessarily equals the group's posttest mean alone. | With the same complete cases, it equals mean posttest minus mean pretest.
Between-group gain difference | A comparison of average change between groups. | An average gain of 7 versus 3 gives a difference of 4. | Between-group gain difference is calculated by ignoring every baseline observation. | Comparing changes differs from comparing final scores alone.
Posttest-only comparison | A group comparison using final scores rather than individual changes. | Final mean scores are compared without subtracting baseline. | With nonequivalent baseline groups, a final-score difference always isolates treatment effect. | Baseline composition can complicate interpretation of final values.
Nonequivalent-group design | A comparison of groups formed without random allocation in the relevant study. | Existing therapy appointments determine group membership. | Measuring pretests makes preexisting groups equivalent by definition. | Pretests reveal some differences without eliminating all measured or unmeasured confounding.
Maturation threat | Natural development over time as an alternative explanation for observed change. | Children gain language skill during the interval regardless of assigned programme. | Every gain between childhood assessments is necessarily due to therapy. | A suitable comparison can help distinguish intervention-related change from expected development.
History threat | Events outside the intervention that influence outcomes during the study. | A new school programme begins while therapy outcomes are measured. | History means only whether the literature review includes old books. | Document concurrent events that might affect the study comparison.
Instrumentation threat | Changes in measurement procedures or instruments affecting observed scores. | A later assessment uses easier prompts than baseline. | Changing the scoring procedure cannot affect apparent treatment gains. | Comparable measurement is necessary to interpret change meaningfully.
Fan-spread pattern | Diverging trajectories where baseline-related growth can mimic a treatment difference. | A group starting higher also improves faster before treatment begins. | Any diverging pre-post lines prove a treatment-specific effect. | Additional pretreatment observations can help examine preexisting growth patterns.
Regression to the mean | The tendency for unusually extreme noisy observations to be less extreme on repeat measurement. | Participants selected for unusually low first scores score closer to average later. | Every movement toward average proves that a treatment caused recovery. | Repeated measures and design evidence help distinguish statistical tendency from genuine change.
Floor effect | Measurement compression near the lower limit of an instrument. | Many participants score zero because the task is too difficult. | A floor effect gives unlimited sensitivity to every further deterioration. | Limited lower range can conceal meaningful differences or changes.
Ceiling effect | Measurement compression near the upper limit of an instrument. | Participants score near maximum before substantial learning is measured. | A ceiling effect proves that no participant can improve in real life. | A restricted instrument can hide change beyond its measurement range.
Responsive outcome selection | Choosing measurement suited to the construct and time scale of expected change. | A targeted probe is evaluated before use over a short therapy interval. | Diagnostic accuracy alone proves sensitivity to all short-term gains. | Assess responsiveness, validity and reliability for the intended purpose.
Criterion-referenced probe | Assessment against specified task criteria rather than only a normative comparison. | A probe measures accuracy on defined target forms. | A clinician-created probe needs no evidence about scoring or measurement quality. | Target fit is useful but does not replace validation and error assessment.
Comparable score units | Consistent measurement meaning and units across repeated observations. | The same interpretable scale is used at pretest and posttest. | Subtracting unrelated instrument scores always yields a meaningful change value. | Confirm measurement comparability before computing or interpreting differences.
Repeated baseline measurement | Collecting multiple observations before intervention introduction. | Three pretreatment probes show whether performance was already rising. | Two endpoints alone completely reveal every person's growth trajectory. | Additional observations can expose trend and instability hidden by endpoints.
Follow-up measurement | A later observation examining persistence or evolution of outcomes. | A probe after a no-treatment interval evaluates retention. | Follow-up can never add information beyond an immediate posttest. | Specify interval and intervening experiences when interpreting persistence.
Statistical power | Probability of rejecting a false null for a specified alternative under assumptions. | A design is planned for 80% power at a meaningful difference. | Power is the probability that the null hypothesis is true. | Power depends on effect, variability, sample size, threshold and design.
Type I error | Rejecting a true null hypothesis within the specified testing framework. | A test signals a difference when the null is actually true. | Type I error means missing every real effect. | Alpha controls a long-run error property under the stated procedure and assumptions.
Type II error | Failing to reject a false null under a specified alternative. | An underpowered study fails to detect a relevant true effect. | A nonsignificant result establishes that Type II error is impossible. | Beta and power concern behaviour under an alternative, not certainty from one outcome.
Standard deviation | A measure of dispersion on the same scale as the observations. | Participants' gain scores vary around their average gain. | Standard deviation is identical to the standard error of the mean. | Spread of individual values differs from uncertainty in an estimated average.
Standard error | Estimated sampling variability of an estimator under a specified model. | A mean estimate has uncertainty based partly on variability and sample size. | Standard error describes only the range of individual participant scores. | Distinguish estimator uncertainty from the distribution of observations.
Independent-groups gain test | A justified comparison of gain values from distinct participant groups. | Two therapy groups' gains are compared under appropriate assumptions. | Gains from different participants always require a paired test merely because each gain uses two scores. | A within-person calculation does not make independent groups paired with each other.
Paired analysis | Analysis accounting for linked observations within the same participant or matched pair. | A within-group pre-post difference is analyzed using paired observations. | A paired within-group result by itself establishes superiority over another group. | Within-group change and between-group treatment difference answer different questions.
Welch t test | A two-mean comparison that does not impose equal group variances. | Independent gain distributions with unequal variances are compared appropriately. | Every t test requires a pooled equal-variance assumption. | Select the test variant according to design and assumptions.
One-way ANOVA | A model comparing group means for one factor under stated assumptions. | A single factor defines three treatment groups. | A significant omnibus result identifies every pairwise difference without further analysis. | An omnibus comparison and specific contrasts require distinct interpretation.
Baseline-adjusted analysis | Modelling follow-up outcomes while accounting for baseline and other justified covariates. | An ANCOVA estimates a conditional group contrast. | Baseline adjustment is forbidden in all nonrandomized studies and automatically unbiased in all randomized studies. | Specify the estimand and assumptions; neither blanket rejection nor automatic causal confidence is justified.
Normality and sample size | A distinction between an observation distribution and sampling-distribution approximations. | A large sample of skewed observations remains skewed as raw data. | Every raw data set becomes normally distributed once n reaches thirty. | Large-sample approximations have conditions and do not transform the observations.
Cohen standardized mean difference | A difference in means expressed relative to a stated standard deviation convention. | A two-point mean difference divided by SD four equals 0.5. | A standardized effect size itself proves meaningful improvement for every client. | State the denominator convention and examine uncertainty and functional relevance.
Hedges g | A standardized mean-difference estimator with a small-sample bias correction. | A correction factor adjusts an appropriate standardized mean difference. | Hedges g differs from Cohen d solely because it uses any pooled standard deviation. | The small-sample correction is central; denominator choices must also be specified.
Individual change versus group average | The distinction between participant-level change and a group summary. | The same mean gain can combine diverse individual outcomes. | A positive group mean proves every participant improved equally. | Inspect distributions and individual patterns instead of relying only on averages.
Functional participation outcome | Measurement of meaningful involvement in everyday communication activities. | A caregiver report complements a targeted grammar probe. | Change on an impairment probe automatically captures every everyday participation benefit. | Different outcome domains can complement one another without being interchangeable.
Sensitivity analysis | Examination of how conclusions change under plausible analytical choices or assumptions. | Alternative justified approaches are compared transparently. | Sensitivity analysis means retaining only the model that produces a significant result. | Report prespecified or justified alternatives rather than fishing for favourable findings.
Illustrative versus empirical evidence | A distinction between teaching examples and observations from real participants. | Artificial tutorial scores illustrate a computation without testing real treatment efficacy. | A tutorial's invented teaching dataset proves that its hypothetical treatment works clinically. | The provided PDF explicitly uses a hypothetical scenario and artificial example data.
''')
